{:edb/db-before-t 1129 :edb/db-after-t 1130 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534442 50 #inst "2026-10-07T04:18:23.074Z" 13194139534442 true] [17592186133544 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/55018a35718d655dc2b0d67692768e7c78ee9c064f137b31435a204280734c08.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of the polynomial $y = p(x)$ shown above, find the solution to $p(x) < 0$." 13194139534442 true] [17592186133544 1100 "Given the sketch of the polynomial $y = p(x)$ shown above, find the solution to $p(x) < 0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/d83a13bf5ff8ffe872e5b3c6e4a0f77b.png)" 13194139534442 false] [17592186133544 1145 "To solve the inequality $p(x) <    0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis. These intervals are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/141519a0d0abc0d1545d940aba1fd52b15e96d81b0c10560960df2710da4937f.png)\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (-3, -1) \\cup (0,2).\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that this is a strict inequality, so we do not include the roots in our solution." 13194139534442 true] [17592186133544 1145 "To solve the inequality $p(x) < 0$, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis. These intervals are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/a34e9e5ebc457ab4ff0ab4ce9004f44f.png)\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (-3, -1) \\cup (0,2)$.\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that this is a strict inequality, so we do not include the roots in our solution." 13194139534442 false] [17592186133545 1100 "Given the polynomial $P(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2)$, find the solution of $P(x) \\geq 0$." 13194139534442 false] [17592186133545 1100 "Given the polynomial $P(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2),$ find the solution of $P(x) \\geq 0.$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186133545 1145 "To solve the inequality\n\n$$\nP(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nP(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2)\n$$\n\nare $x=0$ and $x= \\pm{2}$, where\n\n- $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2$,\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $3$, and\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/6b432fef92363fb20bc112fcae6d7bc7.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n\n$$\nP(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe look for the parts of the curve that are above the $x$-axis or touching the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $[MATH: x \\in (-\\infty,0] \\cup [2, \\infty).]$" 13194139534442 false] [17592186133545 1145 "To solve the inequality $P(x) \\geq 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2)$ are $x=0$ and $x= \\pm{2},$ where\n\n- $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2,$\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $3,$ and\n\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/435846c9b0efa313b76b0e13689fb2225209d7ee1ea52e332a65bd676d97cc8f.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x) \\geq 0,$ we look for the parts of the curve that are above the $x$-axis or touching the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,0] \\cup [2, \\infty).$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186133548 1100 "Given $f(x) = x^3 - 9x$, solve $f(x) \\gt 0$." 13194139534442 false] [17592186133548 1100 "Given $f(x) = x^3 - 9x,$ solve $f(x) \\gt 0.$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186133548 1145 "To solve the inequality $f(x) \\gt 0$, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y = f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{3} - 9x \\\\\n&= x(x^{2} - 9) \\\\\n&= x(x + 3)(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3 - 9x\n$$\n\nare $x=-3$, $x=0$, and $x = 3$, which are all simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/539d1de5f6c1505dc8a8fbe9db23f6b8.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0$, we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-3,0) \\cup (3,\\infty)$." 13194139534442 false] [17592186133548 1145 "To solve the inequality $f(x) \\gt 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y = f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}-9x \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - 9x$ are $x= - 3,$ $x=0,$ and $x = 3,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/91e0e81b98e7f89796c277854bec6ce0240c797fc0c3d1eda3f33ce4bc9c85a6.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx \\in (-3,0) \\cup (3,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186133863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288460 13194139534442 true] [17592186133863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288477 13194139534442 true] [17592186133863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288483 13194139534442 true] [17592186133863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288492 13194139534442 true] [17592186133864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288469 13194139534442 true] [17592186133864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288501 13194139534442 true] [17592186133864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288523 13194139534442 true] [17592186133864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288537 13194139534442 true] [17592186133865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288453 13194139534442 true] [17592186133865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288510 13194139534442 true] [17592186133865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288516 13194139534442 true] [17592186133865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288530 13194139534442 true] [17592186133865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288544 13194139534442 true] [17592186134184 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534442 true] [17592186134184 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/62502a59f15828ddfe1850039930128c0c382b072504725038ecaac9b9b90196.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of a polynomial $p(x)$ above, find the solution to $p(x)<0.$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186134184 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/q-40185.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of a polynomial $p(x)$ above, find the solution to $p(x) < 0$." 13194139534442 false] [17592186134184 1145 "To solve the inequality $p(x)<0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/9b283fb0afe6a6f8bf6950765bbcb845c1262b01c278cc7bdca20b6fe14280ff.png)" 13194139534442 true] [17592186134203 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534442 true] [17592186134203 1100 "Given the polynomial $P(x) = (x + 2)(x - 1)(x - 2)$, find the solution of $P(x) > 0$." 13194139534442 false] [17592186134203 1100 "Given the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2),$ find the solution of $P(x)>0.$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186134203 1145 "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2)$ are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm 2,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/65a899ed4375be4681c0e4598cc4769335cabb52257a886148438f4b4f16e671.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288453 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288454 13194139534442 true] [17592186288453 1089 "q-116437" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288453 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534442 true] [17592186288453 1100 "Given $P(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2},$ solve $P(x)>0.$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288453 1113 #uuid "b1b2fdd9-c0cd-573a-b08c-e85f6aba3027" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288453 1145 "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}$ are $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=\\pm 2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/8916d10a685ad90bdf11c38c0a6e12c8a641a62fd3f0dfde3de644d907b45a8d.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288455 13194139534442 true] [17592186288454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288456 13194139534442 true] [17592186288454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288457 13194139534442 true] [17592186288454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288458 13