 [17592186288544 1089 "q-87672" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288544 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534442 true] [17592186288544 1100 "Given $P(x)=12{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-{x}^{4},$ solve $P(x)>0.$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288544 1113 #uuid "93512429-4252-5f57-bd68-033768354dcc" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288544 1145 "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =12{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-({x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-12) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+3x-12) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}(x(x-4)+3(x-4)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}(x+3)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=12{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}$ are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=4,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=-3$ and $x=4$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/5ebfbd4e18de00fad05c4192be2147a9b9765e00fae5b74ac608e85466c21e73.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,4).\n$$" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288546 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288547 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288548 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288549 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288550 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1041 "selection" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288547 13194139534442 true] [17592186288545 1110 #uuid "ac07d98c-0ca1-51fa-89bd-e9327c978125" 13194139534442 true] [17592186288546 1034 "x\\in (-3,4)" 13194139534442 true] [1759218628